WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.530
- Basically anywhere there are people

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.270
that wanna be heard,
or wanna get something

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.700
off their chest, I bring
Mobile Stü to them,

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.380
and give them the option to either record,

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:19.113
spit some poetry, sing,
rap, self-express, really.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.110
(crowd cheering)

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.120
Mobile Stü is basically a recording studio

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.350
that I started out of my car.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.229
I was trying to solve a problem,

00:00:34.229 --> 00:00:37.130
and that problem was equal access

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:40.720
to musical equipment,
or a recording studio.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.730
Young people from different neighborhoods

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.010
can all access a studio because

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:46.693
I bring Mobile Stü to the people.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:49.230
Yeah, we're going on the street right now.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.910
We're going to the hood.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.100
So when I get to the spot, I pull over,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.330
crack the trunk, get
out all the equipment,

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.540
hook up the microphone, the mic stand,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.140
then they see me pull out the mix board,

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.550
and I start playing beats.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.650
Yo, where my Cypher Heads at?

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.530
We 'bout to get into this?

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:06.910
Everyone starts to come over

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:09.925
and wants to express
themselves on the microphone.

00:01:09.925 --> 00:01:11.376
♪ Boston on the map ♪

00:01:11.376 --> 00:01:14.140
♪ Brand new beats, so
it's a brand new flow ♪

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.850
♪ Four sticks just like a Kit Kat ♪

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:16.730
- And at the same time

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:18.190
that they're doing that, I'm recording it,

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:21.170
so I give them a version
of what they said.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.273
It's like an audible snapshot.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:29.490
So there was this young man
that was murdered in Dorchester.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.600
There's a whole bunch of people that all, like,

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.680
they really wanted to get revenge.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.650
I drove through with Mobile Stü,

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.250
and basically I'm giving them

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.640
the option to express themself.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.080
I kinda realize that Mobile Stü wasn't

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.120
just as simple as I thought it was.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.793
It was de-escalating people,

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.840
and so we're like, "This is de-escalating.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.420
“How can we use this to
de-escalate a national crisis?"

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.400
Which was cops killing kids.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.290
Cops particularly
killing young, black men.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.120
We had the opportunity at that same time

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.020
to come together musically,
which we use Mobile Stü to do.

00:02:05.902 --> 00:02:08.400
♪ Walk with, feel with, let's do it ♪

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.440
- The important aspect of working

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.890
with officers and young people together,

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:14.440
there's a kinda different kind of a bond

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.500
when you know someone not just
as a officer arresting them,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.950
or having to see them when
something bad happens.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.340
Right after all of that
stuff started happening,

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.810
I started getting calls from
the Boston Public Schools.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.420
Walking into an elementary
school is a little bit different

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.330
because we're talking about a classroom

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.050
that was supposed to be the worst class

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.960
in the school at the time, special needs.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.550
Young people that have been
through some serious trauma,

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.870
and I found out that almost all the kids

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.440
in there were musically inclined.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.313
They wanted to be a rapper,
or a singer, or a poet.

00:02:50.215 --> 00:02:52.798
(kids rapping)

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.840
To see a young person
excited about something good

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.970
that they're doing, and I
get to provide that moment,

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.623
that's what's gratifying to me.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.230
These guys deserve to have the opportunity

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.550
to learn how to record.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:11.283
They deserve the ability
to express themselves.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.420
You got this ability
to get yourself heard,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.577
even if it's just the whole
neighborhood in that moment,

00:03:17.577 --> 00:03:20.910
makes people feel value, that something

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.583
like that comes to them.

00:03:25.126 --> 00:03:27.876
(crowd cheering)

